halt -- Command

Stop the machine with every filesystem clean
/etc/halt [ -n ] [ -u ]

/etc/halt is the last thing that runs on the way down.  It unmounts every
filesystem in /etc/mnttab except the root, in reverse table order, syncs twice,
prints

    halt: the machine is stopping -- it is safe to switch off

and calls halt(2), which disables interrupts and stops the processor.  Only
root can run it.

WHAT MAKES A FILESYSTEM CLEAN IS THE SYNC, NOT THE UNMOUNT.  A modified
filesystem carries FSDIRTY in its super block, and the next mount of it is
forced read-only until check(1) has passed over it; the byte is rewritten
FSCLEAN by sync(2), for every mounted filesystem, once its inodes and blocks
are on the disk.  So a filesystem that is still mounted when the machine stops
is as clean as one that was unmounted, provided the last thing to touch it was
a sync -- which is why the sync here is last and why -n has to be asked for.

The unmount is still done first, and done for its own reasons: an unmounted
filesystem cannot be dirtied again between the sync and the stop, and umount(2)
refuses while any process holds a file or a working directory on it, which is a
report about the state of the machine that a bare sync does not give.  A
refused unmount is printed and then ignored:

    halt: /dev/hd6 (/usr) still mounted: in use
    halt: 1 filesystem(s) remain mounted; they are synced

Abandoning the halt there is what would leave the disk dirty, so it does not.

Options
    -n	Do not sync.  Stop the machine as it stands.  Every mounted
	filesystem is then left dirty and will need check(1).
    -u	Do not unmount anything.  Sync and stop.

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it, because there was no such
command: 4.2's /etc/halt sleeps forever and tells the operator to switch off,
the AT it ran on having no way to stop the processor from userland.  This one
is new here and does stop it.

WHAT "STOPS" MEANS DEPENDS ON THE KERNEL.  Without KDDT, halt(2) reaches
halt_ in md.s, which masks interrupts and jumps to itself.  With the in-kernel
debugger linked, that same entry point is the debugger's, and halt(2) RETURNS
when the debugger is told to continue -- so a return is reported rather than
ignored:

    halt: halt(2) returned -- the machine did NOT stop

There is no warm restart on this hardware; see reboot.

